{"name":"Brylcreem","deep":true,"image":{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Brylcreem_3718.jpg/960px-Brylcreem_3718.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","credit":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brylcreem_3718.jpg","caption":"Brylcreem 3718","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},"mentions":0,"casual":{"lead":["Brylcreem is a men's hair styling cream launched in 1928 in Birmingham, England. It is an emulsion of water and mineral oil that gives hair shine and a controlled hold, and its name blends 'brilliantine' and 'cream'.","The appearance payoff is a tidy, glossy, slicked-back or side-parted look in the style of mid-century barbering. It became a household name through the wartime 'Brylcreem boys' and later endorsements."],"sections":[{"h":"How people use it","paras":["A small amount is worked through damp or dry hair and combed into style. Users typically pair it with a comb for slick-backs, side parts and quiffs. The range now also includes gels and waxes in different holds and finishes."],"subs":[]},{"h":"Risks & side effects","paras":["It is a cosmetic product with the usual considerations of scalp or skin irritation for sensitive users. Heavier creams can leave hair looking greasy, which is the look some users want and others avoid."],"subs":[]}]},"gallery":[{"url":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/77/Brylcreem_3718.jpg/960px-Brylcreem_3718.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=thumbnail","full":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/7/77/Brylcreem_3718.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_campaign=imageinfo&utm_content=original","title":"Brylcreem 3718","license":"CC BY-SA 3.0","creator":"Ashley Pomeroy","page":"https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Brylcreem_3718.jpg","source":"Wikimedia Commons"},{"url":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/d/dc/Brylcreem_modern.jpg?utm_source=commons.wikimedia.org&utm_ca